Execute commands

A Terminal is a window/interface that you use to interact with the system.
Bash is a Shell program that runs inside the Terminal, Bash parses commands and tells the sysytem to execute them.


$ echo "hello" > abc.log          //if abc.log doesnt exist, it creates this file; if abc.log exists, it overwrites the file
$ echo "hello" >> abc.log         //add more contents to the bottom of the file

$ ls file1         
ls: cannot access 'file1': No such file or directory      //output
$ ls file1 2> error                                       //write the error message into the error file, overwrite it
$ cat error
ls: cannot access 'file1': No such file or directory      //output
$ ls file1 2>> error                                      //add more contents to the bottom of the file
ls: cannot access 'file1': No such file or directory
ls: cannot access 'file1': No such file or directory      //output

$ ls file1 &> error_and_output                            /redirect both standard output and standard error, the file error_and_output is newly created here                                  
$ ls file1 > output.txt 2> error_and_output               //output standard output and error into different files

$ ls file1 2> /dev/null                                   //throw in the bin
